        ('h3', 'The breadth of the gifts'),
        ('p', '1 Corinthians 12 lists nine gifts: word of wisdom, word of knowledge, faith, gifts of healing, working of miracles, prophecy, discerning of spirits, tongues, and interpretation of tongues. Romans 12 adds further gifts of service, teaching, exhortation, giving, leading and mercy.'),
        ('callout', 'depth', 'The distinction between the nine gifts in 1 Corinthians 12 and the six in Romans 12 matters practically. The Corinthians list leans toward the more overtly supernatural: healing, miracles, tongues. The Romans list includes gifts many believers already exercise without naming them as spiritual gifts at all, such as giving, leading and showing mercy.'),
        ('h3', 'Ministering from supplied ability, not natural skill'),
        ('p', 'Ministering "as with the ability which God supplies" means leaning on God’s strength in the moment, not rehearsed natural skill. This does not exclude natural talent; it means natural talent is not the engine.'),
        ('scripture', 'Hebrews 2:3-4', 'God also bearing witness both with signs and wonders, with various miracles, and gifts of the Holy Spirit, according to His own will.'),
        ('h3', 'Gifts grow through use'),
        ('p', 'Recognising a gift and never stepping out in it leaves it dormant. Paul’s own ministry continued even in difficult circumstances, such as the shipwreck voyage in Acts 27, where he continued to minister faith and encouragement to those around him. Even imperfect first attempts, offered sincerely, are how believers grow into their gifting.'),
        ('callout', 'note', 'You do not need to feel fully confident before you begin. Gifts are sharpened in the exercising of them, not perfected in private before they are ever used.'),
